config CONFIG_FS_MAX_MOUNT_POINT_SIZE
  int "Maximum mount point name length in bytes"
  default 64 
  help 
    This is not maximum mounting in terms of depth, since mounting points are linked list

config CONFIG_FS_MAX_PATH_LENGTH
  int "Maximum path length"
  default 64
  help 
    Maximum path size used inside kernel

config CONFIG_FATFS_CACHE_LINE_SECTORS
  int "Sectors fetched as one run into the FAT metadata cache"
  default 4
  help
    FatFs reads file data straight into the caller's buffer, but reaches
    directory entries and the allocation table one sector at a time. On an SD
    card almost all of a read is the command sequence around it rather than the
    512 bytes, so a sector fetched alone costs roughly ten times a sector
    fetched inside a multi-block transfer. Fetching a run of them amortises
    that, and directory walks are sequential, so the run is used.

    The run cannot grow without limit: the transfer holds interrupts disabled,
    and the console UART has only its 32-byte FIFO to cover the gap -- 0.69 ms
    at 460800 baud. Eight sectors keeps the critical section close to what a
    single-sector read already costs. Set to 0 to disable the cache.

config CONFIG_FATFS_CACHE_LINES
  int "How many such runs are kept"
  default 8
  help
    Total cache size is this times CONFIG_FATFS_CACHE_LINE_SECTORS times 512
    bytes, taken from the kernel heap per mounted FAT filesystem. A sequential
    walk only needs a couple, but the walk is interleaved with reads of the
    allocation table at every cluster boundary, which would otherwise evict the
    directory run each time.

    Keep it modest: the kernel heap is what is left of the 76 KB kernel RAM
    bank. A filesystem that cannot get the memory falls back to reading through,
    so this is a performance setting, never a correctness one.

    Count matters more than length here, because resolving a path touches one
    directory per component and each wants a line of its own. Measured on the
    rig with 4 lines: a file two directories deep cost 1.2 ms cold and 0.2 ms
    on a second open, while one seven deep cost 5.7 ms and did not improve on
    the second open at all -- its levels evicted each other. The default trades
    run length for line count (4 x 8 -> 8 x 4) at the same 16 KiB.

menu "Temporary filesystem (/tmp)"

config CONFIG_TMPFS_ENABLE
  bool "Mount a hybrid RAM/disk filesystem at /tmp"
  default y
  help
    Mount a RamFs at /tmp backed by the board's `temp_ram` region instead of
    letting /tmp be a symbolic link into the SD-backed /root/tmp. Every compile
    writes its output to /tmp and every execute reads it straight back, so both
    become memcpy-speed for the common case.

    Files stay in RAM only while they fit: anything larger than
    CONFIG_TMPFS_MAX_FILE_SIZE, and anything that no longer fits in the arena,
    spills transparently to CONFIG_TMPFS_SPILL_DIRECTORY and behaves exactly as
    it does today. That bound is the point -- an unbounded RamFs at /tmp starved
    the process pool and was a net loss.

    Boards whose memory layout has no Temp-owned region silently keep the
    symlink behaviour.

config CONFIG_TMPFS_MAX_FILE_SIZE
  int "Largest file kept in RAM, in bytes"
  default 65536
  help
    A write that would push a file past this size moves the whole file to
    CONFIG_TMPFS_SPILL_DIRECTORY first, and every later access goes there. Keep
    it well below the arena size so one large file cannot evict everything else.

config CONFIG_TMPFS_ARENA_RESERVE
  int "Arena bytes kept free for filesystem metadata"
  default 8192
  help
    Once free arena space drops below this, a file that wants to grow spills
    instead. Without it the arena can fill with file bodies until there is no
    room left even to create the next file, and /tmp starts answering ENOMEM
    rather than falling back to the spill directory.

config CONFIG_TMPFS_PAGE_SIZE
  int "Arena page size, in bytes"
  default 256
  help
    Allocation granularity of the /tmp arena. Must be a power of two and at
    least 16. Smaller wastes less on file metadata; larger keeps the page bitmap
    and the first-fit scan shorter.

config CONFIG_TMPFS_SPILL_DIRECTORY
  string "Directory holding spilled file bodies"
  default "/root/tmp"
  help
    Must live on a writable filesystem (the SD-backed rootfs on hardware). Files
    here are named yNNNNNNN.tmp and are removed when the /tmp file that owns them
    is unlinked. A reset leaves them behind, but the names restart at y0000001
    and a spill truncates whatever it finds, so the directory holds at most as
    many entries as the run that spilled the most -- it does not grow without
    bound.

endmenu

rsource "ramfs/KConfig"
